# Maestro Command Dispatch

When the user types a Maestro command in chat, load and follow the file in the table below: a skill in `.maestro/skills/` or a command in `.maestro/commands/`.

## Command Recognition

Recognize these patterns (with or without `/` prefix):

| User types | Load file |
|-----------|-----------|
| `mae-explore` or `mex` | `.maestro/skills/mae-explore/SKILL.md` |
| `mae-idea` | `.maestro/skills/mae-idea/SKILL.md` |
| `mae-poc` or `mpoc` | `.maestro/commands/mae-poc.md` |
| `mae-specs` or `msp` | `.maestro/skills/mae-specs/SKILL.md` |
| `mae-requirements` or `mrq` | `.maestro/commands/mae-requirements.md` |
| `mae-design` or `mds` | `.maestro/commands/mae-design.md` (visual design) |
| `mae-architecture` or `mar` | `.maestro/commands/mae-architecture.md` |
| `mae-req` (old name) | `.maestro/commands/mae-req.md` |
| `mae-scope` or `msc` | `.maestro/skills/mae-scope/SKILL.md` |
| `mae-plan` or `mpl` | `.maestro/commands/mae-plan.md` |
| `mae-do` or `mdo` | `.maestro/commands/mae-do.md` |
| `mae-review` or `mrv` | `.maestro/commands/mae-review.md` |
| `mae-init` | `.maestro/commands/mae-init.md` |
| `mae-help` | `.maestro/commands/mae-help.md` |
| `mae-run` | `.maestro/commands/mae-run.md` |
| `mae-yolo` | `.maestro/commands/mae-yolo.md` |
| `sync` | `.maestro/commands/sync.md` |
| `decide` | `.maestro/commands/decide.md` |
| `status` | `.maestro/commands/status.md` |
| `md` | `.maestro/commands/md.md` |

## Dispatch Protocol

1. When a command is recognized, **read the full file** named in the table
2. Pass any arguments the user provided after the command name
3. Follow the command file's behavior specification exactly
4. Save output to the current session folder as specified

## Examples

```
User: "mae-explore the auth system"
→ Read .maestro/skills/mae-explore/SKILL.md
→ Execute with argument: "the auth system"

User: "mex ask client"
→ Read .maestro/skills/mae-explore/SKILL.md
→ Execute with arguments: "ask client"

User: "mrq"
→ Read .maestro/commands/mae-requirements.md
→ Execute with no arguments (smart default)
```

If unsure whether input is a command: if the first word matches a command name above, treat it as a command. Otherwise treat it as a regular message.
